# This file normalizes line endings for text files to LF and protects binary files from any
# line-ending conversion. This matters because Windows and Linux/macOS use different line endings
# by default (e.g. Git and the VS Code Source Control extension can otherwise mark unchanged files
# as modified when switching between them). This file is necessary for release_content.sha256 to
# verify correctly regardless of which OS is used to clone or check out this repository.
# For more info, see https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/remote/troubleshooting#_resolving-git-line-ending-issues-in-wsl-resulting-in-many-modified-files
#
# The base rule below relies on Git's own content-based text/binary detection to apply this
# automatically, with shell and batch scripts exempted since they depend on their own native line
# endings.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.{cmd,[cC][mM][dD]} text eol=crlf
*.{bat,[bB][aA][tT]} text eol=crlf

# The explicit rules below classify common file types as text or binary. Git's automatic detection
# above already handles these correctly on its own; these entries are explicit, self-documenting
# defense against edge cases that could confuse the automatic detection.

